The '''2022 Tauranga by-election''' for the [[New Zealand House of Representatives]] was held on 18 June 2022<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/bay-of-plenty-times/news/tauranga-by-election-prime-minister-jacinda-ardern-announces-date/SOSCPLOFPG2DKDOCSEG23MIDD4/ |title=Tauranga by-election: Prime Minister Jacinda Ardern announces date |date=6 April 2022 |work=[[Bay of Plenty Times]] |publisher=[[The New Zealand Herald]] }}</ref> in the [[Tauranga (New Zealand electorate)|Tauranga electorate]], after the sitting member, former [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] leader [[Simon Bridges]], resigned from parliament.<ref name='resignation'>{{Cite news |title='It's time' – National Party MP Simon Bridges quits, will leave Parliament in coming weeks |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/its-time-national-party-mp-simon-bridges-quits-will-leave-parliament-in-coming-weeks/V3L6FUWZNJXKJQWFD2Q4J5WJ3Y/ |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=15 March 2022 |access-date=15 March 2022 |archive-date=15 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220315030725/https://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/its-time-national-party-mp-simon-bridges-quits-will-leave-parliament-in-coming-weeks/V3L6FUWZNJXKJQWFD2Q4J5WJ3Y/ |url-status=live }}</ref>
The National Party's [[Sam Uffindell]] won the  [[List of New Zealand by-elections|by-election]] in a landslide result, ahead of the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]'s [[Jan Tinetti]].

==Background==
The Tauranga electorate is based around the eponymous city of [[Tauranga]] as well as [[Mount Maunganui]] and [[Omanu Beach]]. It was created in [[1881 New Zealand general election|1881]], disestablished in [[1890 New Zealand general election|1890]] and reestablished in [[1908 New Zealand general election|1908]]. Since the 1908 election the electorate has voted mostly conservatively with the exception of [[1935 New Zealand general election|1935]] wherein [[Charles Burnett (politician)|Charles Burnett]] was voted from the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]. [[Winston Peters]], former [[Deputy Prime Minister of New Zealand|Deputy Prime Minister]], was the Tauranga MP from [[1984 New Zealand general election|1984]] to [[2005 New Zealand general election|2005]] — representing the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]] from 1984 to 1993, at which stage Peters left the party to establish [[New Zealand First]]. In 2005 the electorate was regained for National by [[Bob Clarkson]]<ref>{{cite web|url= https://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2005/electorate-52.html|title=Official Count Results – Tauranga (2005)|accessdate=16 March 2022|website=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]}}</ref> and it has been held by the party since.

In [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] the incumbent MP [[Simon Bridges]] was reelected with a majority of 1,856 votes (4.23%),<ref name="2020 Tauranga">{{cite web|title=Official Count Results – Tauranga (2020)|url= http://electionresults.govt.nz/electionresults_2020/electorate-details-52.html|publisher=[[Electoral Commission (New Zealand)|Electoral Commission]]|access-date=16 March 2022|location=Wellington}}</ref> National's lowest majority in the electorate since 2005.

===Outgoing member===
Bridges took over the electorate from Clarkson in [[2008 New Zealand general election|2008]] and was reelected in every triennial election since. On 15 March 2022, Bridges announced his retirement from politics, thereby causing a by-election.<ref name='resignation'/> Bridges served as a cabinet minister during the [[Fifth National Government of New Zealand]], Bridges was Minister of Economic Development, Minister for Transport, and Minister for Communications.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/members-of-parliament/bridges-simon/|title=Bridges, Simon|accessdate=16 March 2022|website=[[New Zealand Parliament]]|archive-date=26 January 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220126124222/https://www.parliament.nz/en/mps-and-electorates/members-of-parliament/bridges-simon/|url-status=dead}}</ref> Following the 2017 election and the formation of the [[Sixth Labour Government of New Zealand|Sixth Labour Government]] Bridges was elected [[Leader of the New Zealand National Party|National Party leader]] and became [[Leader of the Opposition (New Zealand)|Leader of the Opposition]] between 2018 and 2020, at which stage [[Todd Muller]] [[May 2020 New Zealand National Party leadership election|took over leadership]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300017906/live-simon-bridges-and-todd-muller-in-national-party-leadership-contest|title=Live: Todd Muller beats Simon Bridges in National Party leadership contest|website=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]]|date=22 May 2020|accessdate=16 March 2022}}</ref>

