== Candidates ==
[[New Zealand National Party|National]] in a press release, announced the timeline for candidate nomination submissions, starting from 29 March and ending 13 April. The candidate will be decided on 1 May.<ref>{{Cite web |date=23 March 2022|title=Tauranga candidate nominations open next week |url=https://www.national.org.nz/tauranga-candidate-nominations-open-next-week |access-date=23 March 2022 |website=New Zealand National Party |language=en}}</ref> Rotorua Lakes District Councillor [[Tania Tapsell]] was speculated as a potential candidate. Tapsell unsuccessfully contested the [[East Coast (New Zealand electorate)|East Coast electorate]] in the 2020 election.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newsroom.co.nz/simon-bridges-bows-out-after-fighting-back-to-the-top |title=Simon Bridges bows out after fighting back to the top |date=16 March 2022 |work=Newsroom |first=Jo |last=Moir}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/opinion/300542571/behind-simon-bridges-shock-departure-and-what-comes-next |title=Behind Simon Bridges' shock departure, and what comes next |date=16 March 2022 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |first=Bryce |last=Edwards }}</ref> On 31 March, she said she would not run.<ref>{{cite news |first= Dan |last=Sheridan |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/bay-of-plenty/128273332/it-wont-be-me-tapsell-rules-herself-out-of-tauranga-race |title='It won't be me': Tapsell rules herself out of Tauranga race |date=5 April 2022 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=6 April 2022 }}</ref> Waipā District Councillor [[Susan O'Regan]], daughter of former National MP and Tauranga candidate [[Katherine O'Regan]], ruled out a bid on 7 April.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.teawamutunews.nz/2022/04/oregan-i-wont-stand/ |title=O'Regan: I Won't Stand |date=7 April 2022 |work=Te Awamatu News |first=Mary Anne |last=Gill}}</ref> Former Tauranga City Councillor Kevin Clout said that he was "seriously considering" putting his hand up for the National candidacy.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.nzherald.co.nz/bay-of-plenty-times/news/tauranga-councillor-kelvin-clout-considering-run-at-simon-bridges-tauranga-seat/HD2JBYHLL5PX73JMA6WRNIBMZU/ |title=Tauranga councillor Kelvin Clout considering run at Simon Bridges' Tauranga seat |date=23 March 2022}}</ref> Tauranga Business Chamber CEO and former City Councillor Matt Cowley announced on 13 April that he would seek the National Party's nomination for the election.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.sunlive.co.nz/news/291655-matt-cowley-throws-hat-election-ring.html |title=Matt Cowley throws hat in by-election ring |date=13 April 2022 |work=SunLive}}</ref> On 29 April, the top four National candidates were announced as Clout, Cowley, [[Sam Uffindell]], and Tom Rutherford.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/bay-of-plenty-times/news/tauranga-byelection-top-four-national-contenders-to-replace-simon-bridges-named/YZZL32E7VQNFUED6XIZF6ACVXE/ |title=Tauranga byelection: Top four National contenders to replace Simon Bridges named |date=29 April 2022 |work=[[Bay of Plenty Times]] |publisher=[[New Zealand Herald]] |first=Kiri |last=Gillespie}}</ref> Uffindell was selected on 1 May.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/05/01/sam-uffindell-to-run-for-national-in-tauranga-by-election/ |title=Sam Uffindell to run for National in Tauranga by-election |date=1 May 2022 |work=[[1News]] }}</ref>

Minister of Internal Affairs and Women [[Jan Tinetti]], who contested the electorate at the 2017 and 2020 elections, put her name forward for the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour]] candidacy.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Cooke |first=Henry |date=15 March 2022 |title=Former National Party leader Simon Bridges retiring from politics, will spark byelection |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300541457/former-national-party-leader-simon-bridges-retiring-from-politics-will-spark-byelection |access-date=15 March 2022 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |archive-date=15 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220315030713/https://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/300541457/former-national-party-leader-simon-bridges-retiring-from-politics-will-spark-byelection |url-status=live }}</ref> and was confirmed as their candidate on 6 April.<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.1news.co.nz/2022/04/06/labour-candidate-jan-tinetti-to-run-in-tauranga-by-election/ |title=Labour candidate Jan Tinetti to run in Tauranga by-election |date=6 April 2022 |work=[[1News]] }}</ref>

Former [[Deputy Prime Minister of New Zealand|Deputy Prime Minister]], Tauranga MP and [[New Zealand First]] leader [[Winston Peters]] has been speculated to run for the seat.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Lynch |first=Jenna |title=Winston Peters' ticket back to Parliament? Simon Bridges' resignation to spark Tauranga by-election |language=en |work=[[Newshub]] |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/03/winston-peters-ticket-back-to-parliament-simon-bridges-resignation-to-spark-tauranga-by-election.html |url-status=dead |access-date=15 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220315065620/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/03/winston-peters-ticket-back-to-parliament-simon-bridges-resignation-to-spark-tauranga-by-election.html |archive-date=15 March 2022}}</ref> When asked about potentially running, he replied that it was a "fascinating question", however later stated that he hasn't "had a chance to absorb all that" and doesn't have anything to say at the moment.<ref name=":0" /> On 17 May, he announced that neither he nor his party would stand a candidate in the by-election.<ref>{{Cite web |last=ZB |first=Newstalk |title=Winston Peters rules out a run in next month's Tauranga by-election |url=https://www.newstalkzb.co.nz/on-air/mike-hosking-breakfast/audio/winston-peters-nz-first-leader-rules-out-a-run-in-next-months-tauranga-by-election/ |access-date=16 May 2022 |website=ZB |language=en-nz}}</ref>

The [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]] party board would hold a meeting on the by-election, revealed [[ACT New Zealand|ACT]] Leader [[David Seymour (New Zealand politician)|David Seymour]].<ref>{{Cite news |first1=Dan |last1=Sheridan |first2=Henry |last2=Cooke |date=15 March 2022 |title=Bridges: 'Tauranga by-election will be a straight fight between left and right' |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/bay-of-plenty/128064604/bridges-tauranga-byelection-will-be-a-straight-fight-between-left-and-right |access-date=15 March 2022 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |language=en}}</ref> [[Cameron Luxton]], the previous ACT candidate for the electorate intends to put his name forward for the election.<ref name=":1">{{Cite news |title=Tauranga byelection candidates step forward to fill Simon Bridges' shoes |url=https://www.nzherald.co.nz/bay-of-plenty-times/news/tauranga-byelection-candidates-step-forward-to-fill-simon-bridges-shoes/2X4UFVPLQZY4ENJI3D3GAWJJTA/ |date=17 March 2022 |access-date=17 March 2022 |work=[[Bay of Plenty Times]] |publisher=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |language=en-NZ |first=Sandra |last=Conchie |url-access=subscription }}</ref> Luxton was confirmed as the ACT candidate on 7 April 2022.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/bay-of-plenty/128297242/luxton-to-stand-for-act-in-tauranga-byelection |title=Luxton to stand for ACT in Tauranga by-election |date=7 April 2022 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |first=Dan |last=Sheridan}}</ref>

Peter Wakeman, a [[perennial candidate]] who most often stands in Canterbury and who came third in the [[1993 Tauranga by-election]] with 1.5% of the vote, has said he would stand as an independent candidate.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{cite news |last1=Conway |first1=Glenn |title=Who is Peter Wakeman? |url=https://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/9117566/Who-is-Peter-Wakeman |access-date=20 March 2022 |work=[[The Press]] |date=3 September 2013}}</ref>

Former National MP [[Matt King (politician)|Matt King]] stated that his newly formed [[DemocracyNZ]] party would not be ready in time to contest the election.<ref name=":2" />

The [[New Conservative Party (New Zealand)|New Conservative Party]] announced on 11 April that co-leader Helen Houghton would run for the seat. Houghton contested [[Christchurch East]] previously.<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 April 2022 |title=New Conservative Announces Tauranga Candidate |url=https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/PO2204/S00051/new-conservative-announces-tauranga-candidate.htm |access-date=10 April 2022 |website=www.scoop.co.nz}}</ref>

Former Tauranga City Councillor and [[2019 Tauranga mayoral election|2019 mayoral candidate]] Andrew Hollis will run for the unregistered [[New Nation Party]].<ref name=":2">{{cite news |url=https://thespinoff.co.nz/politics/21-03-2022/the-people-trying-to-turn-the-parliament-occupation-fury-into-a-political-force |title=The people vying to build a political force out of the parliament occupation fury |date=21 March 2022 |work=[[The Spinoff]] |first=Toby |last=Manhire}}</ref>

The [[New Zealand Outdoors Party]] announced on 29 March that co-leader [[Sue Grey (lawyer)|Sue Grey]] would contest the election. Grey previously ran in [[Nelson (New Zealand electorate)|Nelson]].<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.outdoorsparty.co.nz/nz-outdoors-freedom-party-announce-sue-grey-as-candidate-for-tauranga/ |title=NZ Outdoors & Freedom Party announce Sue Grey as candidate for Tauranga |date=29 March 2022 |publisher=NZ Outdoors and Freedom Party}}</ref>

The [[Aotearoa Legalise Cannabis Party]] announced on 31 March that their candidate would be Christopher Coker, who previously contested the [[Bay of Plenty (New Zealand electorate)|Bay of Plenty]] electorate.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.scoop.co.nz/stories/AK2203/S00677/pot-party-to-stand-in-tauranga-by-election.htm |title=Pot Party To Stand In Tauranga By-election |date=31 March 2022 |publisher=Scoop}}</ref>

The [[Māori Party]] announced on 13 May that they would not contest the election for safety reasons, citing claims of threats made by residents.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/05/te-p-ti-m-ori-won-t-stand-candidate-at-tauranga-by-election-due-to-safety-issue.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220512222917/https://www.newshub.co.nz/home/politics/2022/05/te-p-ti-m-ori-won-t-stand-candidate-at-tauranga-by-election-due-to-safety-issue.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=12 May 2022 |title=Te Pāti Māori won't stand candidate at Tauranga by-election due to 'safety issue' |date=13 May 2022 |work=[[Newshub]] |first=Jamie |last=Ensor}}</ref>

